Do not build the bundled gettext when --disable-nls.

gcc 15 carries gettext in its source tree and configures it
unconditionally. --disable-nls stops gcc USING the result; it does not
stop the directory being configured and built.

That is harmless on a GNU/Linux host and is not harmless here. gettext
brings gnulib with it, and gnulib compiled against picolibc wants
things picolibc does not have in the shape it expects: it decides
uselocale() is usable and then calls uselocale(NULL), where this
locale_t is not a pointer; it reaches for C23's ckd_add. Each one stops
the whole build in a library that nothing in this configuration will
ever call, because nothing here is translated.

zlib two lines above is dropped from noconfigdirs by exactly this
mechanism when --with-system-zlib is given. This is the same idea for
the same reason.

--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -2976,6 +2976,18 @@
   noconfigdirs="$noconfigdirs zlib"
 fi
 
+# And don't build the bundled gettext when NLS is off.
+#
+# gcc 15 carries gettext in its tree and configures it unconditionally:
+# --disable-nls stops gcc USING it and does not stop it being BUILT.
+# For a cross build against a small C library that is a long way from
+# free -- its gnulib assumes uselocale() returns a pointer, and reaches
+# for C23's ckd_add - and none of it is wanted, because nothing here
+# is translated.
+if test x$enable_nls = xno ; then
+  noconfigdirs="$noconfigdirs gettext"
+fi
+
 # Don't compile the bundled readline/libreadline.a if --with-system-readline
 # is provided.
 if test x$with_system_readline = xyes ; then
